# OpenFactory — `docker compose` configuration.
#
#     cp .env.compose.example .env.compose
#
# TWO SENSES OF "REQUIRED", and mixing them is what made this file read as contradictory:
#
#   BOOTS   — the stack starts with this file completely empty. Every container comes up, the
#             panel serves, nothing crashes with an unexplained error.
#   RUNS A TICKET — needs exactly TWO of the values below: a forge credential (§1) and an
#             authenticated harness (§2). Nothing else in this file is required, ever.
#
# `openfactory doctor <project>` names whichever of the two is missing, one line each, with the
# remedy — so you can fill this in now or find out from the doctor later. Everything from §3 down
# is a preference: leave it empty and the factory still works.
#
# The full reference (what each variable means, and the ones this file does not carry) is
# docs/reference/configuration.md.
#
# WHY THIS FILE LOOKS GENERIC, AND WHERE YOUR STACK ACTUALLY LIVES. Nothing about Python, Node,
# .NET or Terraform belongs here, and nothing about WHICH vendor you use either — this file holds
# secrets and nothing else. The four decisions live in four different places, on purpose:
#
#   your code's stack ....... `.openfactory/project.yaml`, in YOUR repository — `setup:`,
#                             `validate:`, `components: {stack: python}`. Written by
#                             `openfactory env read/apply`, which reads your repo and proposes it.
#   the box that runs it .... the registry, per project — `box.image` (your CI's own image is the
#                             usual answer). A deployment-wide default ships in docker-compose.yml.
#   which vendors ........... the registry, per project — `tracker/forge/board: {kind: …}`. This
#                             is what decides WHICH rows below you need at all.
#   the secrets ............. this file, deployment-wide, because one deployment can drive many
#                             projects across many vendors and a secret is not a per-project fact.
#
# So: YOU WILL LEAVE MOST OF THIS EMPTY, and that is correct rather than incomplete. Fill the rows
# for the vendors your projects actually use. Better: don't fill this file at all — run
# `openfactory init` and it writes `.env.compose` with ONLY the rows your answers use, obtaining
# what it can (your `gh` login, a panel secret) and naming what it cannot. This template is the
# by-hand fallback and the reference for the rows `init` did not choose.


# ══ 1. WHERE CODE AND TICKETS LIVE — the forge and the tracker ════════════════════════════════
#
# ONE RULE FOR EVERY VENDOR, and it is worth reading once:
#
#     THE PROJECT'S REGISTRY ENTRY NAMES THE VARIABLE. THIS FILE HOLDS THE VALUE.
#
#     registry entry:  tracker: {kind: azure_devops, options: {token_env: ACME_ADO_PAT}}
#     this file:       ACME_ADO_PAT=…
#
# That is what lets ONE deployment drive a GitHub project and an Azure DevOps project side by
# side without either picking up the other's credential. Not hypothetical: with a single
# process-wide token, a worker serving a GitHub project and a Jira project authenticated both
# with whichever one the environment happened to carry, and the Jira board came back with an
# empty queue — a factory that looked quiet instead of broken.
#
# Name nothing in the registry and each axis falls back to the deployment-wide defaults below.
# Fill in the rows for the vendors you actually use; leave the rest empty. The two axes are
# INDEPENDENT — Jira tickets with GitHub code is an ordinary configuration, and so is the
# reverse.

# ── Building on a corporate network ──
# Almost nobody needs these two, and the ones who do cannot build at all without them.
#
# A network that terminates and re-signs outbound HTTPS (Zscaler, Netskope, a corporate proxy)
# breaks every `pip`/`npm` line in the images with CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED. Put your root
# certificate in `docker/extra-ca/` — that is a file, not a variable, and its README explains it.
#
# The SAME networks often throttle port 80, which Debian's mirrors use by design. `apt` then
# streams most of the archive and drops part way through the install, reporting a failed fetch
# rather than a blocked port. Point it somewhere reachable — Debian over https, or your own
# mirror. Empty (the default) fetches where Debian says.
DEBIAN_MIRROR=

# ── GitHub — the deployment-wide default ──
#
# TWO WORDS THIS FILE USES, and they are the two axes above: the FORGE is where the code, the
# branches and the pull requests live; the TRACKER is where the tickets and the board live. On a
# GitHub-only project both are GitHub and one credential covers them.
#
# EITHER a personal access token. Fastest to try, and tied to a person: every commit and PR
# reads as YOU.
#
#   Classic token: github.com/settings/tokens → "Generate new token (classic)"
#     scopes: `repo` and `project`  (add `read:org` if the board lives in an organisation)
#     NEVER `workflow` — its absence is the guardrail that keeps the factory out of your CI/CD
#     definitions. Same reason the App below does not get it.
#   Fine-grained token: grant the repository/organization permissions the App table names —
#     docs/setup/github.md §1.
OPENFACTORY_BOT_TOKEN=
#
# YOU ALMOST CERTAINLY DO NOT NEED THE NEXT TWO — leave them empty and the token above serves
# both axes. They exist for one case: the code and the tickets are on DIFFERENT systems and each
# needs its OWN credential — code on GitHub, tickets on Jira, say. Then this one overrides the
# token above for the code side only…
OPENFACTORY_FORGE_TOKEN=
# …and this one for the ticket side only.
OPENFACTORY_TRACKER_TOKEN=

# OR a GitHub App — its own identity, its own audit trail, and a token that expires. The whole
# walkthrough (both creation pages, the permission table, where each of these three values is
# read, and the smoke test that proves them) is docs/setup/github.md. Two screens, and
# finishing the first LOOKS like finishing: an App reaches no repository until you also
# **install** it. The INSTALLATION id is the number at the end of the URL after Settings →
# GitHub Apps → Configure — it is printed nowhere on the page itself.
#
# Paste the private key CONTENT (the whole `-----BEGIN…END-----` block), not a path — the
# container cannot see your filesystem — and WRAP IT IN DOUBLE QUOTES: unquoted, its line breaks
# corrupt the parse of this ENTIRE file and every other credential silently stops arriving.
# When you use the App, leave OPENFACTORY_BOT_TOKEN above EMPTY — a filled PAT beats the App.
OPENFACTORY_GH_APP_ID=
OPENFACTORY_GH_APP_INSTALLATION_ID=
OPENFACTORY_GH_APP_KEY_CONTENT=""

# ── Azure DevOps — work items → Azure Repos pull requests, with no GitHub anywhere ──
#
# The end-to-end walkthrough (PAT scopes, the two board states you create once, work item
# types, registration) is docs/setup/azure-devops.md.
#
# Default variable name: AZURE_DEVOPS_PAT (override per project with `options.token_env`).
# A PAT from dev.azure.com → User settings → Personal access tokens, with the scopes the guide's
# §1 table names. An `az account get-access-token --resource
# 499b84ac-1321-427f-aa17-267ca6975798` token works too — the adapter accepts either.
#
# The organisation, project and repository are NOT here: they are coordinates, not secrets, and
# `openfactory project add` reads all three out of your dev.azure.com clone URL.
AZURE_DEVOPS_PAT=

# ── Jira — tickets and board (the code can still live on GitHub or Azure Repos) ──
#
# Jira Cloud authenticates as email + API token. The EMAIL and the site are coordinates and live
# in the project's registry entry; only the token belongs here.
# Get it at id.atlassian.com → Security → API tokens.
JIRA_API_TOKEN=


# ══ 2. THE HARNESS — what actually writes the code ════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# THIS IS THE ONE THAT SURPRISES PEOPLE. The platform is free; running it costs whatever your
# harness costs. The CLI has to authenticate INSIDE the box, with no human at a browser — which
# is why a token and not an interactive login.
#
# Claude Code by subscription — run this on your own machine and paste the result:
#
#     claude setup-token
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=
#
# …or an API key instead, if you bill per token rather than by subscription
# (console.anthropic.com → API keys). Set one OR the other, not both.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
#
# Using Codex, Kimi or OpenCode instead? Their credentials are named per harness — see
# docs/reference/configuration.md; the harness itself is chosen in the project's registry entry,
# not here.


# ══ 3. THE PANEL — the surface you and your team actually use ═════════════════════════════════
#
# EMPTY MEANS OPEN: every /api/* route is reachable by anyone who can reach the port. That is
# deliberate for a laptop and wrong for anything else. Any long random string will do:
#
#     openssl rand -hex 32
OPENFACTORY_PANEL_TOKEN=


# ══ 4. OPTIONAL — none of this is needed for your first ticket ════════════════════════════════

# ── The channel: where the tech-lead speaks when nobody is looking at the panel ──
# NOTHING TO FILL IN HERE ON THE CORE, and that is the whole answer: the panel is the channel
# that always exists, so the factory reports on the ticket and takes direction from the panel
# with this section empty. The core reads no chat variable at all — a chat channel arrives as
# an ADD-ON PACKAGE, and the rows it registers declare the variables they read (that package's
# README names them, and `openfactory doctor` prints what an installed row still lacks).
# Add those rows to this file once the package is installed: every row of it reaches the worker,
# which is why the core's own docker-compose.yml names none of them.

# ── The deployment-wide fallback notifier ──
# Where the tech-lead speaks when no project channel can carry it — and where PROJECT-LESS
# notifications go, which otherwise go nowhere. DECLARED as a kind on the notifier axis, never
# inferred from a package's variables: the add-on package that ships the kind names the variables
# its row reads (`telegram`, shipped by openfactory-slack, is one). Leave it unset and the panel
# is the last resort; `openfactory doctor` prints the state as one line.
# OPENFACTORY_NOTIFIER_FALLBACK=

# ── Named people instead of shared tokens ──
# The two variables above (`OPENFACTORY_PANEL_TOKEN`, and `OPENFACTORY_PRODUCT_TOKEN` below) are
# SHARED secrets: whoever holds one is anonymous to the platform, so "who approved that release?"
# has no answer. These give each person their own, as `token:id:display` rows, comma-separated:
#
#     OPENFACTORY_PANEL_TOKENS="tok-ana:ana:Ana Souza,tok-rui:rui:Rui Lima"
OPENFACTORY_PANEL_TOKENS=

# ── The product owner's surface (`/product/<name>`) ──
# A credential for somebody who writes REQUIREMENTS and does not run the floor — a business
# analyst, typically. It reaches the product actions and is refused `merge`, `skip` and the
# production-release gate BY NAME.
#
# The shared form is read-only in practice: every product WRITE checks the person's id against
# `product.admins` in the registry, and a shared token has no id. To let somebody accept, queue
# and release, give them a row in the per-person variable AND list that id in `product.admins`.
# The whole chain is docs/reference/product-role.md.
OPENFACTORY_PRODUCT_TOKEN=
OPENFACTORY_PRODUCT_TOKENS=

# ── Or log in through your own identity provider (SSO) ──
# OpenID Connect ships in the core — Entra ID, Okta, Keycloak, Google and Auth0 all speak it.
# Register the panel at the provider as a web application with the callback
# https://<panel>/auth/callback, then name the row and its coordinates. With this set the token
# variables above are not read: the panel sends a browser to the provider and the id_token it
# issues is the credential. WHO MAY LOG IN is decided at the provider (assign the application
# to the people or groups who may enter); what they reach is decided here — a provider group
# mapped to `product` is scoped to the product surface, any other passes through unchanged so
# a project's `admins` may name it. Full detail: docs/configuration.md.
#
#     OPENFACTORY_IDENTITY=oidc
#     OPENFACTORY_OIDC_ISSUER=https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0
#     OPENFACTORY_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=<client id>
#     OPENFACTORY_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=<client secret — optional, PKCE is used either way>
#     OPENFACTORY_OIDC_REDIRECT_URL=https://<panel>/auth/callback   # behind a TLS-terminating proxy
#     OPENFACTORY_OIDC_GROUPS="OF-Product=product"
OPENFACTORY_IDENTITY=
OPENFACTORY_OIDC_ISSUER=
OPENFACTORY_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
OPENFACTORY_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
OPENFACTORY_OIDC_REDIRECT_URL=
OPENFACTORY_OIDC_GROUPS=


# ══ 5. BRANDING — how the factory signs its work in your repository ═══════════════════════════
#
# `BOT_NAME`/`BOT_EMAIL` are the git author on every commit the factory makes, so they show up in
# your history and your blame view forever. `PLATFORM_NAME` is the title the panel wears.
OPENFACTORY_PLATFORM_NAME=OpenFactory
OPENFACTORY_BOT_NAME=OpenFactory Bot
OPENFACTORY_BOT_EMAIL=bot@openfactory.local


# ══ 6. PORTS — override any that collide with something already running ═══════════════════════
#
# 8080 in particular is contended on nearly every developer machine.
PANEL_PORT=8787
TEMPORAL_UI_PORT=8080
TEMPORAL_PORT=7233
